'''''Campsis'' × ''tagliabuana''''' ('''Madame Galen''') is a mid-19th-century [[Hybrid (biology)#Hybrid plants|hybrid]] between ''[[Campsis radicans]]'' (American trumpet vine) and ''[[Campsis grandiflora]]'' (Chinese trumpet vine). It produces trumpet-shaped, orange to red flowers up to {{convert|3|in|cm|0|abbr=on}} long that appear in loose clusters of 6 to 12. It is a woody, clinging, [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[vine]] that attaches itself to structures and climbs vigorously with [[aerial root]]s like those of ivy. It bears dark-green [[deciduous]] leaves, {{convert|8|-|12|in|cm|0|abbr=on}} long. Its flowers are very attractive to bees, butterflies and birds. Like its parents it is [[hardiness (plants)|hardy]] but in cooler [[temperateness|temperate]] regions requires the shelter of a sunny wall to produce its spectacular flowers in abundance.<ref>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref>

The Latin [[Botanical name#Binary name|specific epithet]] ''tagliabuana'' commemorates the 19th-century Italian nurserymen, Alberto Linneo and Carlo Ausonio Tagliabue.<ref>Visiani, Roberto de. Recensio altera plantarum minus cognitarum quas Hortus Patavinus colit. in: Atti del Reale Istituto veneto di scienze, lettere ed arti. ser. 3. vol. 4. page 135. 1858-1859. [https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/33049#page/141/mode/1up]</ref>

It has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Campsis'' × ''tagliabuana'' 'Madame Galen'|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/97959/Campsis-x-tagliabuana-Madame-Galen/Details | accessdate=15 April 2020}}</ref> 

